17
00:01:04,818 --> 00:01:07,045
So how all those things fit together?

18
00:01:07,481 --> 00:01:12,105
At OpenAI, we host the Flyte for
internal traditional ML training jobs.

19
00:01:12,541 --> 00:01:15,283
The Flyte card shows
the Flyte architecture.

20
00:01:15,553 --> 00:01:19,851
Flyte is an open source, ML orchestration
layer that helps with model training.

21
00:01:20,233 --> 00:01:24,958
So Flyte runs the steps in a workflow
and records the state of each run.

22
00:01:25,328 --> 00:01:29,324
You can think of it as keeping a record
of what the compute actually did.

23
00:01:30,215 --> 00:01:35,012
The middle card is MCP, which
stands for Model Context Protocol.

24
00:01:35,367 --> 00:01:38,803
It's an open standard that lets
an AI application use tools.

25
00:01:39,287 --> 00:01:43,967
A Flyte MCP server can expose a
tool that looks up a run and returns

26
00:01:43,970 --> 00:01:46,517
its task state and recorded errors.

27
00:01:47,442 --> 00:01:49,601
Codex is where I ask the question.

28
00:01:50,030 --> 00:01:51,154
I share the run link.

29
00:01:51,389 --> 00:01:56,303
The Codex calls the Flyte tool, and I
get an answer based on that execution.

30
00:01:56,707 --> 00:01:58,604
The useful handoff is the link.

31
00:01:59,193 --> 00:02:03,360
I can share the same link with a teammate,
and we can start from the same run

32
00:02:04,360 --> 00:02:08,307
A workflow defines the step and
how they depend on each other.

33
00:02:08,690 --> 00:02:11,402
A run is an attempt to
carry out those steps.

34
00:02:11,943 --> 00:02:14,153
Here we have a small image classifier.

35
00:02:14,623 --> 00:02:18,533
It loads a data set, trains the
model, and evaluates it on a

36
00:02:18,542 --> 00:02:20,629
separate split, split of the data.

37
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:23,621
Run forty-one finished all three steps.

38
00:02:23,848 --> 00:02:25,800
Run forty-two stopped in training.

39
00:02:26,088 --> 00:02:27,684
Both came from the same workflow.

40
00:02:28,711 --> 00:02:31,764
That history matters when you
are trying to reproduce a result.

41
00:02:32,131 --> 00:02:36,772
Suppose my-- I open my editor the next
morning, and I've made three more changes.

42
00:02:37,182 --> 00:02:40,238
Which version of the code did
the failed job actually run?

43
00:02:40,575 --> 00:02:43,504
Did it use the same data set
as the last successful run?

44
00:02:43,758 --> 00:02:47,292
Did I change the batch size or did
I give it a different kind of GPU?

45
00:02:47,943 --> 00:02:51,342
I keep the run link and record
the workflow version, the

46
00:02:51,343 --> 00:02:54,191
software environment, the data
set environment, and the input.

47
00:02:54,677 --> 00:02:59,048
For this classifier, that includes the
evaluation split and the batch size.

48
00:02:59,377 --> 00:03:04,348
If I change one thing in the next run,
those records give me a fair comparison.

49
00:03:04,657 --> 00:03:09,179
They also let me trace a model
I want to serve, serve, serve

50
00:03:09,462 --> 00:03:11,035
back to the run that produced it

51
00:03:12,035 --> 00:03:17,422
So the next question is, how
Codex, how Codex asks Flyte to

52
00:03:17,422 --> 00:03:18,745
get the, all the information.

53
00:03:19,331 --> 00:03:22,323
The integration answers
a s- a small question.

54
00:03:22,538 --> 00:03:26,816
Given a link, can Codex tell which
step failed and what Flyte recorded?

55
00:03:27,202 --> 00:03:29,712
That gives the tool a
narrow read-only job.

56
00:03:29,967 --> 00:03:35,442
So when I ask about run 42, the link
tells Codex which execution to look up.

57
00:03:35,791 --> 00:03:39,813
The Flyte MCP tool can return the
run state, the task that failed,

58
00:03:40,010 --> 00:03:41,637
and the available error details.

59
00:03:42,010 --> 00:03:45,751
here, Flyte says that the run
step ran out of GPU memory.

60
00:03:46,092 --> 00:03:50,516
It also tells us evaluation was skipped,
so I can focus on training first.

61
00:03:51,049 --> 00:03:55,590
For a team building a similar integration,
start with the information someone would

62
00:03:55,590 --> 00:03:58,455
normally open the workflow UI to find.

63
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:01,679
Give the tool a way to
identify the exact run.

64
00:04:01,994 --> 00:04:06,145
Return the task and error in a
form that the agent can refer to.

65
00:04:06,611 --> 00:04:11,609
Keep access scoped to the runs, to, to
the runs the caller is allowed to see.

66
00:04:11,941 --> 00:04:16,389
So that, those are the principles
that we follow when designing the MCP.

67
00:04:17,389 --> 00:04:21,796
So Codex CLI is the coding agent
I can use from my terminal.

68
00:04:22,019 --> 00:04:26,544
Once it has the execution detail, I can
ask it to explain the error and help

69
00:04:26,545 --> 00:04:28,160
me find the setting I would change.

70
00:04:28,437 --> 00:04:31,983
In this example, the evidence is
an out of memory error in training.

71
00:04:32,255 --> 00:04:34,765
The suggestion is to try a smaller batch.

72
00:04:35,166 --> 00:04:38,613
A batch is the set of examples
the model processes together.

73
00:04:39,219 --> 00:04:42,333
A smaller batch usually means the
training step needs to keep fewer

74
00:04:42,333 --> 00:04:43,997
